WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
<v Maya>Marcus, your own mother thought you were an assistant.</v>

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:03.680
<v Marcus>She did.</v>

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.580
<v Marcus>Dinner party, a few years ago.</v>

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:12.020
<v Marcus>Someone asked what her son did for a living, and she said, with total confidence: he's an assistant to the CEO.</v>

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.760
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.640
<v Maya>Okay, I need the full story.</v>

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.120
<v Maya>Did you correct her?</v>

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:22.680
<v Marcus>I stopped correcting her.</v>

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.400
<v Marcus>Explaining what a chief of staff does at a dinner party takes longer than the dinner.</v>

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.700
<v Maya>Did that sting?</v>

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:29.940
<v Maya>A little?</v>

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.380
<v Marcus>Honestly, yes.</v>

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.680
<v Marcus>Not because she used the wrong words.</v>

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.100
<v Marcus>Because she was right about the visibility.</v>

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.100
<v Marcus>Nobody sees this job.</v>

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:49.860
<v Marcus>You can sit ten feet from the most powerful person in the company for five years, shape decisions worth millions, and at a dinner party you're still "an assistant to the CEO."</v>

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.140
<v Marcus>That's the deal, and you have to make peace with it or it'll eat you.</v>

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.920
<v Maya>That's funny, but it's also the whole reason this show exists, isn't it?</v>

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.040
<v Maya>Nobody knows what this job is.</v>

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:00.780
<v Marcus>Nobody.</v>

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.880
<v Marcus>And that's the loneliness of it.</v>

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.800
<v Marcus>Most companies have exactly one chief of staff.</v>

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:06.720
<v Marcus>One.</v>

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.200
<v Marcus>No peers down the hall, nobody in the building quite understands your days.</v>

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.140
<v Marcus>So you're out there wrestling with these invisible problems, and there's no one to compare notes with.</v>

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.300
<v Maya>Give me one.</v>

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.900
<v Maya>An invisible problem.</v>

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.980
<v Maya>What does that actually look like?</v>

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.400
<v Marcus>It's nine at night.</v>

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.660
<v Marcus>The board deck is due at eight in the morning.</v>

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:31.800
<v Marcus>Three executives are fighting over one slide, each convinced the other is misrepresenting their numbers.</v>

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.280
<v Marcus>Your principal texts two words: handle it.</v>

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.420
<v Marcus>So you get on the phone, you untangle it, the deck goes out clean.</v>

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.760
<v Marcus>Nobody will ever know you were there.</v>

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.180
<v Marcus>No one thanks you.</v>

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.620
<v Marcus>The meeting goes well and the credit flows everywhere except to you.</v>

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.940
<v Marcus>That is the job, on a Tuesday.</v>

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.280
<v Marcus>And every chief of staff listening just nodded, because they've lived that exact night.</v>

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.600
<v Maya>Your exact Tuesday.</v>

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.420
<v Maya>Okay, I felt that.</v>

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.200
<v Maya>But every job is lonely in its own way.</v>

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.260
<v Maya>What's actually different here?</v>

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.540
<v Marcus>What's different is there's no playbook.</v>

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.120
<v Marcus>There's no degree in chief of staff studies.</v>

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.500
<v Marcus>Nobody grows up dreaming about this.</v>

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:23.300
<v Marcus>People fall into it, from operations, from consulting, from being the executive assistant who kept getting promoted until someone said, congratulations, you're the chief of staff now.</v>

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:24.040
<v Maya>Wait.</v>

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.040
<v Maya>That's actually how it happens?</v>

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.440
<v Marcus>That's how it happens.</v>

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:38.240
<v Marcus>And all the real knowledge, how to read your principal's mood in the first ten seconds of a meeting, how to tell your boss they're wrong without getting fired, all of it just lives in people's heads.</v>

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.060
<v Marcus>Nobody writes it down.</v>

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.580
<v Marcus>So this show is us trying to extract it, one conversation at a time.</v>

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.640
<v Maya>Institutional knowledge for a profession that never wrote anything down.</v>

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.560
<v Maya>Okay, so that's for current chiefs of staff.</v>

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.040
<v Maya>What about people thinking about the job?</v>

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.620
<v Marcus>They're the ones I worry about most.</v>

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:02.000
<v Marcus>Right now there's almost no way to learn what this job actually is before you take it.</v>

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:10.060
<v Marcus>The job descriptions are vague, the interviews are vague, and then you start and, surprise, it's nothing like you imagined.</v>

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.340
<v Marcus>People wash out in six months.</v>

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.240
<v Marcus>Not because they weren't good enough.</v>

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.960
<v Marcus>Because nobody told them the truth first.</v>

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.500
<v Maya>Give me one truth.</v>

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.480
<v Maya>The one nobody says out loud.</v>

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.440
<v Marcus>Your wins belong to your principal.</v>

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.060
<v Marcus>If you need public credit to feel successful.</v>

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.760
<v Maya>This is the wrong job.</v>

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.600
<v Marcus>You're already thinking like one.</v>

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:33.480
<v Marcus>And here's why that breaks people: most high achievers run on recognition.</v>

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:34.700
<v Marcus>It's fuel.</v>

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:41.320
<v Marcus>Take the fuel away and the engine stops, usually around month eight, usually quietly.</v>

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.880
<v Marcus>Nobody tells you that in the interview.</v>

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.820
<v Marcus>But it's the difference between thriving and burning out in year one.</v>

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.420
<v Maya>So recap: you're not alone, even when your own mother doesn't get it.</v>

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.740
<v Maya>Someone is finally writing the playbook down.</v>

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.140
<v Maya>The truth, told before you jump.</v>

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.040
<v Maya>And honestly, a voice for the invisible craft.</v>

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.760
<v Marcus>Invisible doesn't have to mean silent.</v>

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.480
<v Marcus>When this job is done brilliantly, the principal looks brilliant.</v>

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.660
<v Marcus>That's the deal, most of us signed up happily.</v>

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.380
<v Marcus>But naming what great looks like lifts the whole profession.</v>

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.420
<v Marcus>Better chiefs of staff make better executives.</v>

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.040
<v Marcus>Better executives build better companies.</v>

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:20.640
<v Marcus>That's the chain.</v>

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.860
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.180
<v Maya>See you next week!</v>

